WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:09.304
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:17.074
Unit one: Historiography. Today you will explain how history is written and rewritten with the passive + by + agent.

4
00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:19.980
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:24.213
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.423
Primary source.

7
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:32.028
Primary source means a document or object created at the time being studied.

8
00:00:32.617 --> 00:00:35.928
A soldier's diary is a valuable primary source.

9
00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:39.610
Your turn. Say: Primary source.

10
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:43.746
Primary source.

11
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:45.960
Historical bias.

12
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:51.399
Historical bias means distortion caused by a writer's perspective or interests.

13
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:55.539
Court chronicles often show historical bias toward the king.

14
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.046
Your turn. Say: Historical bias.

15
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.563
Historical bias.

16
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:05.411
Revisionism.

17
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:10.262
Revisionism means the reinterpretation of accepted historical views.

18
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.568
Revisionism can correct myths or, at worst, deny facts.

19
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:17.929
Your turn. Say: Revisionism.

20
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:21.930
Revisionism.

21
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:24.091
Periodization.

22
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.464
Periodization means dividing the past into named periods.

23
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.094
The term 'Angkor period' is a form of periodization.

24
00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:35.556
Your turn. Say: Periodization.

25
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.668
Periodization.

26
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:41.418
Causation.

27
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:46.216
Causation means the relationship between causes and effects.

28
00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:49.977
Historians debate the causation of the empire's decline.

29
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.143
Your turn. Say: Causation.

30
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.024
Causation.

31
00:01:57.783 --> 00:01:59.191
Interpretation.

32
00:02:00.183 --> 00:02:03.762
Interpretation means an explanation of what past events mean.

33
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.325
Each generation offers a new interpretation.

34
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:10.697
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

35
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.790
Interpretation.

36
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:16.911
Archival research.

37
00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:22.215
Archival research means study of original records kept in archives.

38
00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:25.466
Archival research revealed forgotten letters.

39
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.248
Your turn. Say: Archival research.

40
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.531
Archival research.

41
00:02:34.283 --> 00:02:35.691
Contextualization.

42
00:02:36.683 --> 00:02:41.318
Contextualization means placing a source within its historical circumstances.

43
00:02:41.917 --> 00:02:45.948
Contextualization prevents us from judging the past anachronistically.

44
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.748
Your turn. Say: Contextualization.

45
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.031
Contextualization.

46
00:02:54.783 --> 00:02:55.494
Archive.

47
00:02:56.483 --> 00:02:59.400
Archive means a collection of historical records.

48
00:03:00.017 --> 00:03:02.910
The national archive holds colonial-era maps.

49
00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:05.821
Your turn. Say: Archive.

50
00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:09.540
Archive.

51
00:03:10.283 --> 00:03:11.284
Testimony.

52
00:03:12.283 --> 00:03:15.212
Testimony means a formal personal account of events.

53
00:03:15.817 --> 00:03:18.641
Survivors' testimony was recorded on film.

54
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.833
Your turn. Say: Testimony.

55
00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:25.598
Testimony.

56
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.155
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:31.017 --> 00:03:35.791
Visal, why do two textbooks describe the fall of Angkor so differently?

58
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:45.886
Because history is often reinterpreted by later generations. The older book was written by colonial scholars who blamed a 'decadent' court.

59
00:03:46.983 --> 00:03:48.182
And the newer one?

60
00:03:49.283 --> 00:03:58.902
It was shaped by archaeologists using satellite data. They argue that the decline was caused largely by climate change and failing water systems.

61
00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:02.492
So which interpretation should we trust?

62
00:04:03.583 --> 00:04:13.480
The one best supported by evidence, after contextualization. Every account is written by someone, and their purpose must always be considered.

63
00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:16.296
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.427
Grammar focus: Passive with by plus agent.

65
00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:21.855
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.757
History is often reinterpreted by later generations.

67
00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.857
The older textbook was written by colonial scholars.

68
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.493
The new theory was shaped by satellite evidence.

69
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:44.203
Include 'by + agent' only when the agent is important or new information. In historiography it often matters a great deal, because who wrote an account shapes what it says.

70
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.413
Be careful! Don't say: The history was rewritten from later scholars.

71
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:54.458
Say: The history was rewritten by later scholars.

72
00:04:54.758 --> 00:04:57.001
Use 'by' for the agent of a passive.

73
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.336
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:02.634
The History of History.

75
00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:06.977
Historiography is the study of how history has been written, and by whom.

76
00:05:07.967 --> 00:05:13.426
Every account of the past is produced by an author with a particular perspective, purpose and audience.

77
00:05:14.433 --> 00:05:22.145
Colonial histories of Cambodia, for instance, were frequently written by European officials who saw Khmer culture through imperial assumptions.

78
00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:30.021
Later interpretations have been strengthened by archival research, archaeology and the testimony of local communities.

79
00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:37.096
Historiography therefore reminds us that history is not simply the past, but a continuing argument about it.

80
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.167
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:43.017 --> 00:05:45.864
The theory was challenged blank new evidence.

82
00:05:46.164 --> 00:05:49.434
A, from. B, by. C, with by.

83
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.674
The answer is B: by.

84
00:05:55.817 --> 00:05:58.327
A diary written during a war is a blank.

85
00:05:58.627 --> 00:06:02.965
A, primary source. B, periodization. C, revisionism.

86
00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.196
The answer is A: primary source.

87
00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:13.789
Placing a source within its historical circumstances is blank.

88
00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:18.695
A, causation. B, testimony. C, contextualization.

89
00:06:22.233 --> 00:06:24.767
The answer is C: contextualization.

90
00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:27.533
Choose the correct passive.

91
00:06:27.833 --> 00:06:35.086
A, The chronicle wrote by monks.. B, The chronicle was written by monks.. C, The chronicle was wrote by monks..

92
00:06:38.633 --> 00:06:41.933
The answer is B: The chronicle was written by monks..

93
00:06:43.083 --> 00:06:47.335
According to the reading, historiography reminds us that history is…

94
00:06:47.635 --> 00:06:54.493
A, a continuing argument about the past. B, simply a list of dates. C, written only by officials.

95
00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:01.647
The answer is A: a continuing argument about the past.

96
00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:05.869
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:10.237
Why do historians sometimes interpret the same event differently?

98
00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.492
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:28.792 --> 00:08:03.180
Historians interpret the same event differently because every account reflects the available sources and the historian's perspective. The fall of Angkor, for example, was once explained by colonial scholars as moral decline, whereas recent research emphasises climate change and water management. New evidence, such as satellite imaging, can transform our understanding. Some find this frustrating, but I believe debate, tested against evidence, is how historical knowledge improves.

100
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.108
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:06.017 --> 00:08:10.294
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive with by plus agent.

102
00:08:10.594 --> 00:08:15.511
Today's value is historical awareness. Know the past so you can understand the present.

103
00:08:15.811 --> 00:08:18.289
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

104
00:08:18.589 --> 00:08:21.032
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

